= Preserved baseline capture: supervisor duplication hypotheses, 2026-08-04 =

Provenance
  repo commit at capture : 8b8cc7d (fix(watch): keep supervision alive across delivered wakes and parked lanes)
  captured               : 2026-08-04, before any change in this branch
  host                   : Linux 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2
  probes                 : h1-lock-path-identity.sh, h2-release-by-path.sh, h3-prelock-evicts.sh
                           (this directory; each takes the repo root as its one argument)

These three probes decide the three unproven hypotheses recorded in the incident
record for the endogenous supervisor duplication of 2026-08-04. They are kept
because a reproduction that exists only in a transcript cannot be re-verified by
anyone who did not run it, and because the fix they justify must stay falsifiable
after the fix lands: re-running them against a pre-fix tree must still reproduce
the outputs below, and the regression suite proves the post-fix tree does not.

--- H2: does a dying predecessor's release-by-path unlink a SUCCESSOR's lock? ---
$ tests/fixtures/supervisor-singleton/h2-release-by-path.sh "$PWD"
predecessor pid=19680 ownerdir=.probe.lock.owner.8Q8kXR link=.probe.lock.owner.8Q8kXR
after rotation: link=.probe.lock.owner.gsQKXS holder_pid=99999
RESULT: H2 KILLED - successor lock survived predecessor release-by-path

  Verdict: KILLED. fm_lock_release resolves the symlink to the owner directory it
  currently points at and releases only when THAT owner's pid file names the
  releasing process. After a rotation it names the successor, so the predecessor's
  EXIT trap returns without unlinking. Release-by-path is not the defect.

--- H3: does fm-watch.sh's PRE-LOCK work evict the live incumbent? ---
$ tests/fixtures/supervisor-singleton/h3-prelock-evicts.sh "$PWD"
incumbent W1=26728 holds the singleton
RESULT: H3 PROVEN - incumbent W1 was terminated by W2's PRE-LOCK migration
  W1 output:
  W2 output:
  lock now held by: none (W2=26921)

  Verdict: PROVEN, and stronger than the hypothesis stated. The pre-lock step is
  not merely a widened window: bin/fm-pr-check-migrate.sh, run by bin/fm-watch.sh
  BEFORE its singleton gate, reads the watch lock's pid, verifies its identity,
  and SIGTERMs it. A newcomer that has passed no singleton gate therefore
  terminates the incumbent as its first act, deciding from process inspection
  exactly what the standing rule says must never be decided that way. The early
  exit at "migration already complete" is the only thing that normally keeps this
  path dormant; any state that makes the scan incomplete re-arms it.

--- H1: is the singleton keyed by an ADDRESS rather than a home IDENTITY? ---
$ tests/fixtures/supervisor-singleton/h1-lock-path-identity.sh "$PWD"
--- B1: same physical dir, different path strings ---
live watchers on the same physical state dir: 1 (expect 1)
  B1: singleton HELD across differing path strings
--- B2: one logical home, two state addresses ---
live watchers across two state addresses: 2 (2 = address-keyed, not identity-keyed)
  process table shows: 7 matching 'fm-watch.sh'
  lock A holder=43802 home=/home/jamada/.treehouse/firstmate-8c3a87/4/firstmate
  lock B holder=43804 home=/home/jamada/.treehouse/firstmate-8c3a87/4/firstmate

  Verdict: PROVEN at the mechanism level, and sharper than the hypothesis stated.
  B1 kills the easy half: a symlinked or dot-segmented address for the SAME
  physical state directory still resolves to the same lock file, so ordinary path
  variation does not split the singleton.
  B2 proves the real defect. Two watchers whose state directories differ each
  take their own lock and both survive - and both locks record the SAME
  fm-home. The lock RECORDS a home identity it is never KEYED by, and nothing
  cross-checks it, so one home can host two processes that each correctly believe
  they are its only supervisor. An address is not an identity.
  The third line is the other half of the same defect: a process-table pattern
  scan counted seven matches for two watchers, because the probe harness and its
  arguments carry the pattern too. Counting supervisors by matching text is
  biased toward exactly the processes working on the problem.

--- What stays unknown ---
Which of these fired during the original 2026-08-04 incident is NOT decided by
these probes and is not decided by any durable record that existed at the time.
state/.watch-cycle-exits.log records arm and watcher pids and lock identity, but
never the resolved home or state address of the cycle, so the record cannot
distinguish "two cycles in one home" from "one cycle each in two homes". That
gap is treated as a defect in the instrument, not as evidence in either
direction: the fix records the resolved home identity per cycle so the same
question is answerable from the record next time.
